WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.730
You do not need to fire a single shot to starve a city; you only need to close the roads.

2
00:00:05.730 --> 00:00:12.580
When every gate into Berlin was shut in the summer of nineteen forty-eight, what was left were three thin corridors in the sky.

3
00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:18.630
There were sixty-two seconds until the next aircraft could land — and that lasted fifteen months.

4
00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:32.560
In the summer of nineteen forty-eight, Berlin was a strange thing for anyone looking at a map.

5
00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:41.810
The four powers that had won the war had divided the city into four parts among themselves, but the city itself sat right in the belly of the Soviet occupation zone,

6
00:00:41.810 --> 00:00:47.220
more than a hundred and sixty kilometres inside the nearest occupation boundary to the west.

7
00:00:47.220 --> 00:00:53.430
So the American, British and French sectors were not on the edge of a country; they were in the middle of a sea.

8
00:00:53.430 --> 00:01:03.480
Every sack of flour, every ton of coal, every barrel of petrol that entered those sectors had to travel over railways, roads and canals under Soviet control.

9
00:01:03.480 --> 00:01:14.650
When this arrangement was set up at Potsdam in nineteen forty-five, nobody counted it as a danger; the Allies were still in the mood of the war they had won together, and they intended to govern Germany together.

10
00:01:14.650 --> 00:01:18.940
Three years later, nothing of that intention was left.

11
00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:30.750
The Allied Control Council, which was supposed to govern the city and the country jointly, broke apart in practice in March nineteen forty-eight; the Soviet representative walked out of a meeting and never returned.

12
00:01:30.750 --> 00:01:34.400
But what really accelerated the rupture was not politics.

13
00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:36.290
It was money.

14
00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:41.540
The Reichsmark left over from the war, the old German mark, meant nothing.

15
00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:50.150
Because prices were frozen on paper, the shops were empty, real trade was done on the black market, and the real currency was the American cigarette.

16
00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:57.480
To get out of this dead end, the Western powers decided to tie their zones into a single economy and issue a new currency.

17
00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:05.290
They announced it on the eighteenth of June, nineteen forty-eight, and put it into force on the twenty-first of June: the Deutsche Mark.

18
00:02:05.290 --> 00:02:16.140
Two hundred and fifty million new marks, printed in America and carried secretly into Germany, changed prices, shop windows and expectations across the western half of the country overnight.

19
00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:22.670
The Soviets issued a separate currency for their own zone and treated the whole of Berlin as that currency's territory.

20
00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:27.760
On the twenty-third of June the Western powers brought the new mark into the western sectors.

21
00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:30.450
The answer came the next morning.

22
00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:41.460
On the twenty-fourth of June, nineteen forty-eight, all passenger and freight traffic to the western sectors stopped, for "technical reasons." A bridge on the motorway went in for repairs,

23
00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:46.230
a fault appeared on the railway, maintenance began on the canal.

24
00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:52.280
The electricity supplied to the western sectors from power stations in East Berlin was cut off.

25
00:02:52.280 --> 00:02:58.490
The potatoes, milk and vegetables that came in from the surrounding villages stopped as well.

26
00:02:58.490 --> 00:03:07.100
The western sectors had thirty-six days of food and forty-five days of coal in their stores, and more than two million people were living off those stores.

27
00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:11.550
In the Western capitals, three courses of action were discussed that week.

28
00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:23.360
The first was to drive an armoured convoy up the motorway and force the barriers open; the American military governor, General Lucius Clay, argued for this, while Washington thought it might end in war.

29
00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:29.570
The second was to evacuate the city; to Clay, that would be a letter of surrender handed to the rest of Europe.

30
00:03:29.570 --> 00:03:34.660
The third was the option nobody regarded as a serious solution: the air.

31
00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:39.750
Here, a small piece of paper written at the end of the war turned out to be decisive.

32
00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:51.880
In November nineteen forty-five, the four powers had agreed in writing on three air corridors running from western Germany to Berlin: three lanes approaching from the direction of Hamburg, Hanover and Frankfurt,

33
00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:54.730
each of them thirty-two kilometres wide.

34
00:03:54.730 --> 00:04:01.420
Rights of passage on the ground had never been tied to any such document; rights of passage in the air had been.

35
00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:10.510
Throughout the blockade the Soviets left those corridors alone; to touch them would have meant disregarding their own signatures and accepting the risk of direct conflict.

36
00:04:10.510 --> 00:04:18.480
Clay telephoned Curtis LeMay, the commander of the American air forces at Wiesbaden, and asked whether his aircraft could carry coal.

37
00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:23.570
As the exchange has come down to us, LeMay's answer was that they could carry anything.

38
00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:33.300
On the twenty-sixth of June, nineteen forty-eight, the American transport aircraft in Europe, twin-engined C forty-seven Dakotas, took off for Berlin.

39
00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:37.110
Eighty tons of cargo were set down in the city that day.

40
00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:44.600
Two days later the British opened their own route; Berliners called the whole business the Luftbrücke, the air bridge, and the name stuck.

41
00:04:44.600 --> 00:04:52.730
But the gulf between the eighty tons of that first day and what the city actually needed was itself the real problem that the coming months would have to solve.

42
00:04:52.730 --> 00:04:58.460
Because "feeding a city from the air" is a romantic sentence, but a concrete calculation.

43
00:04:58.460 --> 00:05:02.430
The planners sat down and did the arithmetic.

44
00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:14.080
Keeping Berliners from starving meant something like fifteen hundred tons of food a day: flour and grain, dried potato, powdered milk, fat, meat, sugar, salt, and a little coffee.

45
00:05:14.080 --> 00:05:23.170
On top of that, heating, electricity generation and keeping the factories turning required some three and a half thousand tons of coal and liquid fuel.

46
00:05:23.170 --> 00:05:27.460
The total came to a figure close to five thousand tons a day.

47
00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:32.870
The aircraft on hand was the C forty-seven, which carried three tons per flight.

48
00:05:32.870 --> 00:05:37.480
That meant well over a thousand flights a day, and it was not possible.

49
00:05:37.480 --> 00:05:49.290
So the first piece of the solution was technical: four-engined C fifty-four Skymasters, which carried ten tons and were easier to unload, were pulled into Germany from American bases all over the world.

50
00:05:49.290 --> 00:05:55.500
The British thickened the route too, with York transports and with aircraft from the civilian companies they chartered.

51
00:05:55.500 --> 00:05:59.950
The second piece was the airfields, and that was where matters turned ugly.

52
00:05:59.950 --> 00:06:05.520
Tempelhof airport, in the American sector, was right inside the city.

53
00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:15.890
There were apartment blocks seven storeys high on the approach line; aircraft passed over the rooftops and came down at a steep angle, and at the end of the runway stood a cemetery wall.

54
00:06:15.890 --> 00:06:24.340
The runway surface had not been built for this work either: thin asphalt laid over rubble fill cracked under ten-ton aircraft landing one after another.

55
00:06:24.340 --> 00:06:32.470
German work crews would run onto the runway in the minutes between landings, fill the holes with sand and asphalt, and then pull back to the edge.

56
00:06:32.470 --> 00:06:39.000
Gatow airport, in the British sector, was in better shape, but its capacity was limited.

57
00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:44.570
On the twenty-eighth of July, nineteen forty-eight, General William Tunner arrived in Berlin.

58
00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:53.820
Tunner had run the supply route flown over the Himalayas to China in the Second World War; that route had been won not with heroics but with a timetable,

59
00:06:53.820 --> 00:06:59.390
an industrial process in which every lost aircraft was entered as a line in a ledger.

60
00:06:59.390 --> 00:07:03.520
Tunner saw air supply not as an adventure but as a factory.

61
00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:10.050
In his own words, a successful air bridge in operation was about as exciting as water dripping onto stone.

62
00:07:10.050 --> 00:07:15.620
The day that proved how right he was came on Friday, the thirteenth of August, nineteen forty-eight.

63
00:07:15.620 --> 00:07:22.630
The weather was foul: low cloud had settled over Berlin, it was raining, visibility kept dropping.

64
00:07:22.630 --> 00:07:29.000
Tunner had set out that day for a ceremony in the city, and his aircraft joined the queue above Tempelhof.

65
00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:36.330
First, at the end of the runway, a C fifty-four came down faster than it should have, went out of control and caught fire.

66
00:07:36.330 --> 00:07:44.940
The pilot behind him, dropping out of the cloud and seeing an aircraft burning on the runway, braked hard; his tyres blew and he closed the runway.

67
00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:50.190
The third aircraft turned for the auxiliary dirt strip and skidded off it.

68
00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:54.800
Three minutes of confusion turned into a disaster that would last an hour.

69
00:07:54.800 --> 00:08:01.010
Above Berlin, aircraft were stacked in layers up to three thousand metres, circling inside the cloud.

70
00:08:01.010 --> 00:08:05.300
The tower was wrestling with several voices on the radio at once.

71
00:08:05.300 --> 00:08:14.070
And from the bases in the west, a new aircraft was taking off every three minutes and entering the same cloud, because nobody had told them to stop.

72
00:08:14.070 --> 00:08:19.160
Tunner switched on the radio, identified himself and silenced the tower.

73
00:08:19.160 --> 00:08:25.850
Then he took that stack apart one aircraft at a time: everyone would return to their departure base with their cargo.

74
00:08:25.850 --> 00:08:29.180
For an hour, Berlin received nothing.

75
00:08:29.180 --> 00:08:35.870
Luck helped that day and nobody was killed, but as he circled overhead Tunner saw one thing with complete clarity.

76
00:08:35.870 --> 00:08:40.480
The problem was neither the courage of the pilots nor the number of aircraft.

77
00:08:40.480 --> 00:08:44.930
The problem was that each of two hundred aircraft was making its own decisions.

78
00:08:44.930 --> 00:08:52.260
Every pilot flew at his own speed, judged the weather with his own eyes, attempted the approach according to his own experience.

79
00:08:52.260 --> 00:08:57.350
In good weather this worked; in bad weather the entire system jammed at the same moment.

80
00:08:57.350 --> 00:09:01.800
What would feed Berlin was not more aircraft, but fewer decisions.

81
00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:09.930
What Tunner built after Black Friday was a structure that had never been attempted in the history of aviation: turning the sky into an assembly line.

82
00:09:09.930 --> 00:09:12.460
The logic was simple.

83
00:09:12.460 --> 00:09:16.750
If every aircraft does the same thing, none of them has to think.

84
00:09:16.750 --> 00:09:20.080
First he removed the weather from the realm of debate.

85
00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:29.490
Whether the sky was overcast or brilliantly clear, every flight would be flown under instrument rules; under no circumstances would a pilot decide by eye.

86
00:09:29.490 --> 00:09:36.820
Then he made the speed uniform: in the corridor everyone would fly at the same speed, around two hundred and seventy kilometres an hour.

87
00:09:36.820 --> 00:09:44.630
There was no place for a slow aircraft or a fast one; this was one of the reasons the C forty-sevens were withdrawn from the route.

88
00:09:44.630 --> 00:09:52.600
The corridors were divided into five layers a hundred and fifty metres apart, and each aircraft moved in its own layer, in its own minute.

89
00:09:52.600 --> 00:09:56.570
Aircraft were lined up nose to tail, three minutes apart.

90
00:09:56.570 --> 00:10:01.180
And the hardest rule of all: every aircraft had a single right of approach.

91
00:10:01.180 --> 00:10:07.550
A pilot who missed the runway would not try again; he would turn back to his base with his cargo.

92
00:10:07.550 --> 00:10:15.040
One aircraft's second attempt disrupted the sequence of dozens behind it; one flight was sacrificed and the line was preserved.

93
00:10:15.040 --> 00:10:19.490
Waiting over Berlin, stacking up, was forbidden outright.

94
00:10:19.490 --> 00:10:22.820
The ground section of the line was even more exacting.

95
00:10:22.820 --> 00:10:28.390
The target was for an aircraft to spend no more than half an hour between landing and taking off again.

96
00:10:28.390 --> 00:10:36.200
The crews did not get out; fuel, the weather report, the signatures, all of it came to the aircraft.

97
00:10:36.200 --> 00:10:47.690
Tunner had mobile snack bars selling coffee and sandwiches loaded onto jeeps and sent to the edge of the runway, right up to the nose of the aircraft, and these snack bars were run by young women from Berlin;

98
00:10:47.690 --> 00:10:52.460
what eliminated a crew's wish to walk to the canteen was the canteen coming to them.

99
00:10:52.460 --> 00:11:04.910
The unloading was done by German teams of twelve, working for money and for meals; a competition for records broke out among them, and the ten-ton load of a C fifty-four came to be taken off in under ten minutes.

100
00:11:04.910 --> 00:11:13.520
Maintenance fell into the same discipline: an inspection every fifty flying hours, and heavy maintenance every two hundred hours at the Burtonwood base in England.

101
00:11:13.520 --> 00:11:18.930
Aircraft now went into the workshop not when they broke down, but when the schedule said so.

102
00:11:18.930 --> 00:11:24.340
The cargo itself was redesigned too, because every kilogram meant a kilogram of fuel.

103
00:11:24.340 --> 00:11:29.430
Instead of potatoes they carried dried potato; Berlin had water.

104
00:11:29.430 --> 00:11:32.600
Instead of milk, powdered milk went.

105
00:11:32.600 --> 00:11:36.090
The bone was stripped out of the meat.

106
00:11:36.090 --> 00:11:47.580
Coal was loaded in army duffel bags made from surplus military cloth; the dust of that coal got into everything, into the aircraft's radios, into their wiring, into the crews' lungs.

107
00:11:47.580 --> 00:11:51.230
Salt was a harder cargo, because it ate metal.

108
00:11:51.230 --> 00:12:02.080
For that reason the British carried salt in Sunderland flying boats taking off from the Elbe; those aircraft, with hulls built to withstand sea water, landed on Lake Havel in the west of the city.

109
00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:08.930
When the lake froze over in winter, the salt was loaded into containers slung under the bellies of bombers.

110
00:12:08.930 --> 00:12:14.820
To understand what this machine was capable of, one had to wait for the spring of nineteen forty-nine.

111
00:12:14.820 --> 00:12:26.310
In mid-April, for the sake of morale and competition, Tunner staged a planned maximum-effort trial; for twenty-four hours, from the fifteenth to the sixteenth of April, the whole route carried nothing but coal.

112
00:12:26.310 --> 00:12:35.880
The number of flights reached one thousand three hundred and eighty-three, twelve thousand nine hundred and forty-one tons of coal came down in Berlin, and there was not a single accident.

113
00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:42.090
That worked out to one landing, across all the airfields, every sixty-two seconds on average.

114
00:12:42.090 --> 00:12:46.700
A city's sky had become a shift line running at intervals of less than a minute.

115
00:12:46.700 --> 00:12:54.830
After that day the daily average never fell back to its old figures; nine thousand tons was now what an ordinary working day was worth.

116
00:12:54.830 --> 00:12:59.760
But a landing every sixty-two seconds was not possible with two airfields.

117
00:12:59.760 --> 00:13:06.450
Underneath that record lay a third runway, dug largely by hand in the autumn of nineteen forty-eight.

118
00:13:06.450 --> 00:13:10.740
Tempelhof and Gatow had hit their ceiling by the autumn.

119
00:13:10.740 --> 00:13:19.510
The only place to look for a third airfield was the French sector, and there, in the north-west of the city, stood an old artillery training ground: Tegel.

120
00:13:19.510 --> 00:13:24.280
The first pick struck the ground on the fifth of August, nineteen forty-eight.

121
00:13:24.280 --> 00:13:28.730
There were no machines on that site; there were people.

122
00:13:28.730 --> 00:13:35.900
Over the following months nineteen thousand Berliners worked on the job, women and men, three shifts, day and night.

123
00:13:35.900 --> 00:13:41.310
Most of the workers were women, because they had already been clearing the city's rubble for years.

124
00:13:41.310 --> 00:13:50.560
The wage was a few western marks a week and one hot meal a day; in the winter of the blockade, everyone knew that the second item was worth more than the first.

125
00:13:50.560 --> 00:13:59.970
The fill for the runway came out of the city itself: the bricks of bombed houses were broken up, sieved, and poured under a runway of roughly seventeen hundred metres.

126
00:13:59.970 --> 00:14:04.420
Berlin built the ground that aircraft would land on out of its own ruins.

127
00:14:04.420 --> 00:14:15.110
The one thing that could not be solved was heavy machinery; there were no graders, rollers or asphalt pavers in the city, and none of them would fit through the door of any transport aircraft.

128
00:14:15.110 --> 00:14:25.960
In workshops in western Germany the machines were cut into pieces with welding torches, carried into Berlin piece by piece in wide-bodied C eighty-twos, and welded back together at the edge of Tegel.

129
00:14:25.960 --> 00:14:31.850
On the fifth of November, nineteen forty-eight, the first C fifty-four landed on that runway.

130
00:14:31.850 --> 00:14:37.900
Ninety days had passed between the first pick striking the ground and the landing gear touching it.

131
00:14:37.900 --> 00:14:40.910
One problem remained with the new runway.

132
00:14:40.910 --> 00:14:45.200
Two tall radio masts stood directly above the approach line.

133
00:14:45.200 --> 00:14:55.570
The masts were inside the French sector, but they belonged to Berliner Rundfunk, Berlin Radio, which was under Soviet administration, and the broadcasts went out from there.

134
00:14:55.570 --> 00:15:05.300
The French commander, General Jean Ganeval, asked for the masts to be moved, offering to have the Allies cover the cost; his request was refused.

135
00:15:05.300 --> 00:15:11.670
On the morning of the sixteenth of December, nineteen forty-eight, French engineers blew the masts up.

136
00:15:11.670 --> 00:15:19.480
When the Soviet officer who learned of it asked how he could possibly have done such a thing, Ganeval's answer was short: with dynamite.

137
00:15:19.480 --> 00:15:25.370
Even so, the real surprise of these four hundred and sixty-two days was not in the aircraft.

138
00:15:25.370 --> 00:15:33.500
The Soviet plan rested on the assumption that a city under pressure would side with its own stomach rather than with the Western powers; in the eastern sector,

139
00:15:33.500 --> 00:15:38.590
food distribution open to residents of the western sectors was even announced.

140
00:15:38.590 --> 00:15:41.280
What was expected did not happen.

141
00:15:41.280 --> 00:15:51.170
On the ninth of September, nineteen forty-eight, three hundred thousand people gathered in front of the ruins of the Reichstag, the old German parliament building burnt out in the war.

142
00:15:51.170 --> 00:16:04.420
Berlin's elected mayor, Ernst Reuter, addressed not the crowd but the world: the peoples of America, of Britain, of France, of Italy should look at this city and see that they could not abandon it, or these people.

143
00:16:04.420 --> 00:16:09.350
The same shift happened on a smaller scale at the fence around Tempelhof.

144
00:16:09.350 --> 00:16:21.320
In July nineteen forty-eight an American pilot, Gail Halvorsen, shared two packets of gum among the children pressed against the fence watching the aircraft, and told them he would be passing again the next day;

145
00:16:21.320 --> 00:16:25.450
so that they would recognise him, he would rock his wings on the approach.

146
00:16:25.450 --> 00:16:32.620
He made little parachutes out of handkerchiefs, tied sweets and gum to them, and dropped them out of the window on the landing run.

147
00:16:32.620 --> 00:16:42.030
Word spread both in Berlin and in America; boxes of sweets and handkerchiefs began arriving from schools and factories, and the thing turned into an official relief operation.

148
00:16:42.030 --> 00:16:50.640
The children of Berlin named those aircraft the Rosinenbomber, the raisin bombers, and Halvorsen's own name became Uncle Wiggly Wings.

149
00:16:50.640 --> 00:16:56.210
These look like charming details, but the rest of that winter was not charming at all.

150
00:16:56.210 --> 00:17:07.700
In November and December nineteen forty-eight the fog that settled over Berlin very nearly stopped the air bridge; on some days the number of aircraft able to land in the city was limited to a handful,

151
00:17:07.700 --> 00:17:12.950
the coal ration was cut, and people burnt their furniture and the trees in the parks.

152
00:17:12.950 --> 00:17:24.120
Electricity was supplied for only a few hours a day, and a household's turn often fell after midnight; Berliners set their alarms for two in the morning and got up to cook, to iron and to heat water.

153
00:17:24.120 --> 00:17:29.690
Three and a half years earlier, aircraft of the same types had dropped bombs on this city.

154
00:17:29.690 --> 00:17:35.260
Now a city that listened to that same sky counted the days on which it did not hear engines.

155
00:17:35.260 --> 00:17:41.470
The calculation that had started the blockade cracked in two places at once in the spring of nineteen forty-nine.

156
00:17:41.470 --> 00:17:44.480
The first was economics.

157
00:17:44.480 --> 00:17:55.650
Against the blockade imposed on them, the Western powers had built a quiet counter-blockade: shipments of steel, machine parts, timber and chemicals from the eastern zone to the west came to a halt.

158
00:17:55.650 --> 00:18:04.420
East German industry depended on those parts from the west, and the longer the blockade lasted, the more the side under real pressure began to change.

159
00:18:04.420 --> 00:18:06.310
The second was the winter.

160
00:18:06.310 --> 00:18:13.000
The expectation in Moscow had been that the air bridge would collapse of its own accord in the autumn fog and the winter cold.

161
00:18:13.000 --> 00:18:20.170
The fog did indeed strike, but the line did not break; after the winter, the daily tonnage multiplied instead of falling.

162
00:18:20.170 --> 00:18:24.620
Berlin was now better fed than it had been at the start of the blockade.

163
00:18:24.620 --> 00:18:28.430
The first sign came in January nineteen forty-nine.

164
00:18:28.430 --> 00:18:38.160
In written answers to the questions of an American news agency, Stalin made no mention at all of the currency question he had until then set as the condition for lifting the blockade.

165
00:18:38.160 --> 00:18:41.650
Washington noticed that silence and probed it.

166
00:18:41.650 --> 00:18:45.780
In New York, in a corridor of the United Nations building,

167
00:18:45.780 --> 00:18:54.870
a brief conversation that began between the American representative Philip Jessup and the Soviet representative Yakov Malik turned into months of secret bargaining.

168
00:18:54.870 --> 00:19:04.440
The result came into force one minute after midnight on the clock showing the twelfth of May, nineteen forty-nine: the roadblocks came away, the barriers lifted,

169
00:19:04.440 --> 00:19:12.090
and at the Helmstedt crossing a convoy of lorries that had been waiting for days switched on its headlights and turned east, towards Berlin.

170
00:19:12.090 --> 00:19:17.180
The moment the street lamps in the city came back on, people poured out into the streets.

171
00:19:17.180 --> 00:19:19.870
But the aircraft did not stop.

172
00:19:19.870 --> 00:19:27.840
The Western commanders knew that the blockade could be reimposed one morning on the same grounds, and they wanted a safety stock to build up in the city.

173
00:19:27.840 --> 00:19:36.290
The air bridge continued until the thirtieth of September, nineteen forty-nine; from beginning to end it lasted four hundred and sixty-two days.

174
00:19:36.290 --> 00:19:45.700
In that time two hundred seventy-eight thousand two hundred and twenty-eight flights were made to Berlin and two million three hundred and thirty-four thousand tons of cargo were set down;

175
00:19:45.700 --> 00:19:52.870
roughly three quarters of it was carried by American aircraft, the remainder by the British and the civilian companies they chartered.

176
00:19:52.870 --> 00:19:56.680
More than two thirds of the cargo carried was coal.

177
00:19:56.680 --> 00:20:07.210
The cost was recorded as well: a hundred and one people died, most of them in accidents; forty British, thirty-one Americans, one Australian and fifteen German civilians.

178
00:20:07.210 --> 00:20:11.340
The blockade's real legacy, though, took shape outside Berlin.

179
00:20:11.340 --> 00:20:21.870
While the blockade was still running, the North Atlantic Treaty was signed in Washington on the fourth of April, nineteen forty-nine; what lifted the idea of a permanent military alliance,

180
00:20:21.870 --> 00:20:27.600
debated for years in western Europe, out of hesitation was in large part what had happened in Berlin.

181
00:20:27.600 --> 00:20:38.130
On the twenty-third of May, nineteen forty-nine, the Basic Law came into force and the Federal Republic of Germany was founded; on the seventh of October, nineteen forty-nine,

182
00:20:38.130 --> 00:20:42.740
the German Democratic Republic was proclaimed in the east.

183
00:20:42.740 --> 00:20:53.110
The move launched in order to push the Western powers out of Berlin made the division of Germany permanent, turned the western sectors into the emblem of an alliance, and, in the eyes of Berliners,

184
00:20:53.110 --> 00:20:59.000
transformed the occupying armies that had won the war into protectors within three and a half years.

185
00:20:59.000 --> 00:21:05.850
The city drew its own lesson as well: never again would it depend on somebody else's power station for electricity.

186
00:21:05.850 --> 00:21:16.540
In a separate series of flights that began on the sixth of April, nineteen forty-nine, the steel and turbine parts of a power station in Spandau were cut down into sizes that could be carried,

187
00:21:16.540 --> 00:21:24.190
flown in across five hundred and eighty flights as one thousand four hundred and sixteen tons of cargo, and reassembled on site.

188
00:21:24.190 --> 00:21:36.000
The station came on line on the first of December, nineteen forty-nine, with sixty megawatts of output; it was Reuter who pressed the switch, and after his death in nineteen fifty-three the plant took his name.

189
00:21:36.000 --> 00:21:42.850
The runway dug by hand proved long-lived too: Tegel served as Berlin's main airport until twenty twenty.

190
00:21:42.850 --> 00:21:48.580
In front of Tempelhof today stands a three-pronged concrete monument reaching into the sky.

191
00:21:48.580 --> 00:22:00.390
Berliners call it the Hungerkralle, the hunger claw; the three prongs represent the three air corridors, the only legal route into the city, and the names of those who lost their lives are inscribed on its base.

192
00:22:00.390 --> 00:22:06.280
What that monument records is the proof that a city can be fed not by roads but by a timetable.

193
00:22:06.280 --> 00:22:17.130
That the answer to a blockade could be given with arithmetic rather than with tanks was noted by both sides; for forty years Berlin remained the most sensitive point of the Cold War, divided, walled in,

194
00:22:17.130 --> 00:22:19.660
but never besieged again.

195
00:22:19.660 --> 00:22:23.150
Nöron History'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

196
00:22:23.150 --> 00:22:26.800
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
